# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: macroman-encoding-name — Mac Roman encoding names resolve to :MACROMAN, which SBCL does not register
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-macroman-encoding-name

* Context

=cp1252-codec-host-independent.issue= established the rule this issue
applies: the codec set a Common Lisp implementation ships is its own
business, and it must not leak into the language. There it was a
missing CODEC (CCL has no CP-125x), repaired with a single-octet
fallback whose tables are built from babel. Here it is a missing
SPELLING, and the same repair is not available.

Reported by pjb 2026-08-16, loading BricsCAD V26's =vle-extension.lsp=
through ALPM on macOS:

#+begin_example
_$ (alpm-load-system "bricscad-v26-mac-api" nil)
DBG> Error at ALPM-ERROR line 69 col 3 — ALPM: loading
  bricscad-v26-mac-vle:vle-extension
  (/Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/vle-extension.lsp) failed:
  AutoLISP builtin LOAD signaled an error:
  Undefined external-format: :MACROMAN
#+end_example

* Problem

Measured on this machine (SBCL 2.6.3, CCL 1.13, babel 20260101):

| spelling    | SBCL | CCL | babel |
|-------------+------+-----+-------|
| =MAC-ROMAN= | yes  | yes | no    |
| =MACINTOSH= | yes  | yes | no    |
| =MACROMAN=  | *no* | yes | no    |
| =MAC_ROMAN= | no   | no  | no    |

babel ships no Mac Roman table at all (its set is ASCII, CP-125x,
CP-437, CP-932, EBCDIC, GBK, EUC-JP, ISO-2022-JP, ISO-8859-*, KOI8-*,
KSC-5601, Unicode). So the single-octet fallback in
=autolisp-reader/source/external-format.lisp= — which asks babel for
its 256-entry table — has nothing to build from, and cannot cover a
host that lacks the codec. Normalising the NAME is the only repair.

Both name-resolution paths landed on the one keyword SBCL lacks:

- =NORMALIZE-ENCODING-NAME= (=autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=),
  behind OPEN's third argument, squeezes dashes / underscores / spaces
  out of the name before interning it — so =mac-roman=, =MAC_ROMAN= and
  =macroman= ALL became =:MACROMAN=. The squeezing is what created the
  bug: every spelling a user might type collapsed onto the single
  spelling SBCL does not register.
- =PARSE-LOCALE-ENCODING-STRING= (=autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp=),
  behind LOAD's positional =[encoding]= argument, knew only
  utf-8 / latin-1 / cp1252 / ascii and interned everything else
  upper-cased — same destination.

Observed before the fix, with no ALPM involved:

#+begin_example
_$ (open "f" "r" "mac-roman")   ; [enc-unsupported-target], nil
_$ (open "f" "r" "MAC_ROMAN")   ; idem
_$ (open "f" "r" "macroman")    ; idem
_$ (open "f" "r" "macintosh")   ; works — the only spelling that did
#+end_example

The same AutoLISP program therefore worked on a CCL build of clautolisp
and failed on an SBCL build: a host artefact leaking into the language.

This is not a niche spelling. BricsCAD on macOS reports
=(getvar "SYSCODEPAGE")= as ="MAC_ROMAN"= (read-only, see
=issues/closed/cad-chr-vs-loaded-encoding.issue=), so

#+begin_src lisp
(setq *AUTOLISP-FILE-ENCODING* (getvar "SYSCODEPAGE"))
#+end_src

— the obvious portable idiom — was broken on macOS for SBCL builds.

* Proposal

One alias table for encodings whose spelling differs across hosts, in
=autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp=:

#+begin_src lisp
(defparameter *encoding-spelling-aliases*
  '(("MAC-ROMAN" :mac-roman "MACROMAN" "MACINTOSH" "XMACROMAN" "CP10000")))
#+end_src

with =SQUEEZE-ENCODING-NAME= (upcase; drop =-=, =_=, space) and
=RESOLVE-ENCODING-SPELLING-ALIAS= (returns canonical name + keyword),
consulted by all three paths that turn a user-supplied encoding name
into a keyword — the same three-site lesson as
=cp1252-codec-host-independent=, where fixing OPEN alone would have
left the CLI and LOAD broken:

1. =PARSE-LOCALE-ENCODING-STRING= — LOAD's positional argument;
2. =NORMALIZE-ENCODING-NAME= — OPEN's third argument, checked BEFORE
   the CP-NNNN branch so =CP-10000= lands on Mac Roman instead of the
   unknown =:CP10000=;
3. =RESOLVE-ENCODING-NAME= — the CLI's =-e= / =-E=, so =-e macroman=
   stops being a usage error on SBCL while working on CCL.

The table is deliberately NOT the general alias registry
(=autolisp-cli:*ENCODING-ALIASES*=, the mandatory four): Mac Roman is
neither mandatory nor backfillable. A host shipping no spelling at all
must still get the existing =enc-unsupported-target= diagnostic rather
than a substitute codec we cannot honestly provide.

* Acceptance criteria

1. [X] =(open path mode ENC)= accepts =mac-roman=, =MAC-ROMAN=,
   =macroman=, =MACROMAN=, =mac_roman=, =MAC_ROMAN=, =macintosh=,
   =x-mac-roman=, =CP-10000= and =r,ccs=MAC_ROMAN=, all → =:MAC-ROMAN=.
2. [X] =(load path ENC)= accepts the same set — the ALPM report's path.
3. [X] =-e= / =-E= accept the same set and publish canonical
   ="MAC-ROMAN"= in =*AUTOLISP-{FILE,TERMINAL}-ENCODING*=; the
   line-terminator suffix still composes (=-e macroman-mac= →
   =(:mac-roman :newline :cr)=); a typo (=mac-romain=) is still a
   usage error.
4. [X] Each of the three suites asserts that the emitted keyword is one
   =EXTERNAL-FORMAT-AVAILABLE-P= accepts on the running host, so a host
   that drops the spelling fails the suite instead of a user's LOAD.
5. [X] User manual documents the accepted spellings and the
   SYSCODEPAGE idiom (=clautolisp-user-manual.org=, "Encoding names the
   hosts spell differently"); RELEASE_NOTES entry added.
6. [ ] ALPM stops emitting ="macroman"= — see below. (Outside this
   repository; tracked here because this issue is where the diagnosis
   lives.)

* Resolution

Fixed in 1.8.44. Tests: =autolisp-runtime/tests/model-tests.lisp=,
=autolisp-builtins-core/tests/builtin-tests.lisp=,
=autolisp-cli/tests/cli-launch-tests.lisp=.

** Outside this repository

The name that triggered the report came from ALPM, which canonicalised
=mac-roman= / =macintosh= to ="macroman"= — a name no Common Lisp
registers — and passed it as LOAD's encoding argument:

- =alpm/sources/alpm.lsp= — =*alpm--encoding-aliases*= /
  =*alpm--known-encodings*=: canonical name changed to ="mac-roman"=.
- =alpm-systems/scripts/generate-cad-vendor-systems-macos.py= — emitted
  Python's codec name (=mac_roman= → ="macroman"=); now ="mac-roman"=.
- The generated =alpm-systems/bricscad-v26-mac/*.alpm= files.

clautolisp accepts every spelling either way now; the ALPM change is so
that the FILE FORMAT carries a name that means something outside Python.

** Deferred

Should =*ENCODING-SPELLING-ALIASES*= grow the other Mac code pages
(=mac-cyrillic=, =mac-greek=, =mac-iceland=, all of which SBCL ships)?
Nothing has asked for them, and a row is worth adding only once a host
divergence is MEASURED rather than assumed. Left out on purpose.
